  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 242 AM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nThru Sat Night...Numerous to widespread showers over the local \ncoastal waters early this morning, with strongest activity off of \nthe central/north Brevard coasts and streaming northwest onshore. \nThru Sat, an additional 2-4\" nearly areawide (esp north of\nOkeechobee County/Treasure Coast) will be possible, except 4-6\" \nacross the Volusia/Brevard coasts, with High End additional \nrainfall 1-in-10 (10%) chance exceeding 6-8+\". Locally heavy \nrainfall falling on already saturated ground exacerbating ongoing \nflooding in coastal areas across Volusia & Brevard. Primary \nconcern over the next 24-48 hours will continue to be locally \nheavy rainfall and (flash) flooding. Do not drive through flooded \nroadways if you encounter flooding!\n\nA HIGH risk (level 4 out of 4) for excessive rainfall will exist \ntoday along the Space/Volusia coasts, with Slight/Moderate risks \n(levels 2/3 out of 4) further south and across the interior. While\nrain chances finally decrease on Sat (25-50%) a Marginal risk \n(level 1 out of 4) will exist from near Indian River County \nnorthward.\n\nSurface observations show a broad area of low pressure has developed \nnear southeast FL, northwest Bahamas, and the adjacent coastal \nwaters. Uncertainty remains if this system can obtain subtropical or \ntropical characteristics (50% chance) while it ventures N/NNE along \nto just offshore the FL east coast thru Sat. \n\nNortheast winds (10-20 mph, gusts to 20-30 mph) develop across much \nof the area, except for coastal Volusia, mainland north Brevard, and \nnorth Brevard barrier islands where the pgrad will be tighter and \nwinds should increase here to 15-25 mph with gusts to 35 to 40 mph \ninto tonight. As such, have hoisted a Wind Advisory for these areas \nbeginning at 12Z/7AM this morning and going until 12Z/7AM Saturday \nmorning. Will allow future shifts to monitor and re-address should \nstronger winds also develop a bit further south along the coast or \npoints further inland. \n\nHigh temperatures generally in the L70s-L80s (south) with lows in\nthe 60s, except U50s possible N/W of I-4 Sun morning.\n\nA High Surf Advisory remains in effect through Sat as winds and seas \nbuild over the local coastal waters. Rough, pounding surf will \npromote moderate to locally major beach erosion along the coast. In \naddition, a HIGH rip current threat will continue. Entering the \nrough and dangerous surf is strongly discouraged!\n\nSun-Thu...Aloft, troughiness moves away leaving zonal flow in its \nwake into Mon, then additional shortwave energy will traverse the FL \npeninsula into mid-week ahead of a vigorous upper low digging across \nthe Deep South/north Gulf. At the surface, former low pressure off \nof the Carolina coast will move up the Eastern Seaboard with drier\nair filtering down the peninsula behind it. A weak \"back-door\" \nfront will press across the central FL peninsula on Mon becoming \nquasi-stationary, though we keep PoPs absent from the forecast \nmostly until Tue/Wed (20-30%) as moisture slowly increases back \nacross the area. A low (15-20%) chance for lightning south of \nOrlando these two days. Max temperatures will mostly range from \nthe M70s to L80s (south) into Wed, then U60s to L70s across the \nI-4 corridor on Thu, with M70s near Lake Okee. Mins generally in \nthe L-M60s thru Wed morning, except U50s possible for areas \nnorth/west of I-4. On Thu morning L-M50s possible N/W of I-4.",
